"if not, on the contrary."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(conjunction)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Don't forget to always include the closing tags, otherwise, the browser may not be able to properly read the document."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "furthermore"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "in addition, moreover."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Furthermore, when inserting a link, you will be entering the source of the link after the \u2018href' attribute."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mix up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to confuse something with something else."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Our teacher didn't tell us what the difference between tags, elements, and content was and that's why I was mixing them up when practicing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "nested "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that is placed inside something else."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: No, she didn't tell us that all HTML documents consisted of nested HTML elements."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n8le01ob03aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about elements and attributes in HTML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a list of the most important aspects about elements and attributes in HTML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a summary with all the information you found."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read the summary to your class and have a little discussion about it."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read about elements and attributes in HTML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I summarize important information of a particular topic."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write a text about elements and attributes in HTML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about important aspects of HTML."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Future Continuous in Affirmative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use the <strong>future continuous</strong> to talk about actions that will be in the processes of occurring in the future."}, {"type": "text", "content": "We can use the <b>future continuous</b> in different situations:</br></br>  <ol><li> When projecting ourselves into the future.</br>  Example: <i>Tonight, we <b>will be practicing</b> how to write an HTML document.</i> </li></br>  <li>When predicting something that can happen in the future. </br> Example: <i>Next month, we <b>are going to be presenting</b> our webpage to the rest of the class. </i> </li></br>   <li>When talking about continuous events that will take place in the future. </br> Example: <i>When we write the final HTML document, we <b>will be inserting</b> all the necessary attributes. </i> </li></ol></br>  Don't forget that we can use both the modal verb <b>will</b> and <b>be going to</b> for the future continuous as shown in the previous examples. </br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'll be practicing HTML tonight, but I missed the last class. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "So, when you practice tonight, you're going to be writing elements inside other elements."}, {"type": "example", "content": "This means that you'll be entering a new tag and closing it before a previous one has been closed. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Alright, you'll be using attributes when you want to add extra information to an element."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Reported Speech in Negative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We can use the <strong>reported speech</strong> when we want to inform what someone said or did not say."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Just as we can use the <b>reported speech</b> to inform what someone said, we can also use it to express what someone <b>did not say</b>. </br></br> The same rules apply for both the affirmative and <b>negative</b> <b>reported speech</b>:  <ul><li>The <i>verb tense</i> of the <b>reported sentence</b> depends on the tense that introduces the reported sentence. </li> <li>We generally use the relative pronoun <i>that</i> to introduce the <b>reported sentence</b></li> <li>We do not use quotation marks. </li></ul></br> The only difference between the affirmative form and the <b>negative form</b> is that we use the <b>negative form</b> of the verb that introduces the <b>reported statement</b>. </br></br>  Example: <i>The teacher <b>did not say that</b> we had to finish the document by tomorrow.</i></br></br> Don't forget the <i>shift tense</i> that needs to be taken into account when reporting from <b>direct</b> to <b>reported speech</b>:"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\"><strong>DIRECT SPEECH</strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\"><strong>REPORTED SPEECH</strong></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present (facts)</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple present</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Simple past</div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div align=\\\"center\\\">Present continuous </div>