n the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 3, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob03re2img01,jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<b>Annie</b>: I'll be practicing HTML tonight, but I missed the last class. Did you talk about HTML elements?</br><b>Matt</b>: Yes. To help you catch up, an HTML element is basically what goes from the beginning of an opening tag to the end of the closing tag, including the content that's written in between. </br><b>Annie</b>: Our teacher didn't tell us what the difference between tags, elements, and content was and that's why I was mixing them up when practicing. I'm currently following a complementary course online and they talked about nested elements. Did you address this last class?</br>"}, {"time": "40000", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<b>Matt</b>: No, she didn't tell us that all HTML documents consisted of nested HTML elements. Nevertheless, we learned this when doing our homework. So, when you practice tonight, you're going to be writing elements inside other elements. This means that you'll be entering a new tag and closing it before a previous one has been closed. For example, you'll insert tags for headings and paragraphs inside the <  html  > and < / html >. Don't forget to always include the closing tags, otherwise, the browser may not be able to properly read the document. </br><b>Annie</b>: That's what I learned in the virtual course. However, they didn't say that we could add attributes to tags, and I don't think I'm fully aware of what they are. </br>"}, {"time": "97500", "image": "enpe7n8le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<b>Matt</b>: Alright, you'll be using attributes when you want to add extra information to an element. For instance, when inserting the <  html > tag, you can add a language attribute for specifying the language the document is written in. Furthermore, when inserting a link, you will be entering the source of the link after the \u2018href ' attribute. And the same goes for images, where attributes like \u2018 src ' or \u2018 width ' provide additional information on the source and size of that image.</br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n8le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n8le01ob03re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}